nodecode-kokpit is a rewrite of sahindemirbas's sahindemirbas/kokpit:

  https://github.com/sahindemirbas/kokpit
  commit 21112512fcb8d5fa24efc1558e9b50195eb031a0
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Sahin Demirbas (LICENSE)

The rewritten plugin is integrations/hermes/kokpit of that commit: a Hermes
Agent plugin that keeps a plain Markdown vault, captures notes, tasks and
journal lines into it from a chat, reads Threads.md, To-Do.md and
Last-Session.md back, writes a session card and a short receipt when a session
produced something, and runs a night pass whose proposals each quote the vault
line they came from.

What was rewritten, in Common Lisp against Nodecode's cell contract: the vault
formats the plugin reads and writes (Threads.md blocks with their "Open:"
items and on you / Sende / Bende owner marks and their wiki-link sources,
To-Do.md with its Open-style heading, its checkboxes and its due: dates,
Last-Session.md cards split on the middle dot, inbox notes with their front
matter, daily journal lines), the layout resolution that takes a setting, then
a file at the vault root, then the one match found below the vault and refuses
two, append-only capture (a note is a new file that never overwrites, a task is
one line under the Open heading, a journal line is added at the end of the
day's file), the session card and the receipt that point at the files the
session wrote, a word search over the vault's Markdown, and the night pass
with its two rule-driven proposals (a thread that has not moved for
stale_days, a task due within three days or late), each carrying the path, the
line and a verbatim quote, accepted only by appending a To-Do line. The
plugin's tool names are kept (kokpit_brief, kokpit_brain_search,
kokpit_threads, kokpit_capture, kokpit_night) and its /kokpit subcommands
become one /kokpit command and one manual topic.

What was left out, and why: everything that needs the Kokpit application's
SQLite database read-only -- the calendar, weather, events, job listings, AI
token usage, job health and training-log views, whose tables the original
reads through a schema-tolerant layer -- because this cell is the vault half
the plugin's own README says works without the app; the optional host-model
night pass (night_model), which sends vault excerpts to the model and which
the original keeps off by default, because the rules alone answer offline; and
the dashboard tab, the desktop pane and the optional server module
(disk, load, temperature and systemctl --user), all of them the app's or the
machine's surface rather than the vault's. The demo vault the original copies
when nothing is configured is replaced by a refusal that names the vault
setting. See README. LICENSE is the original repository's LICENSE, unchanged.
